so how does it work and crucially how do  you win  well the national vote doesn't determine  who sits in the white house  each state is allocated a set number of  electors  based partly on its population called  the electoral college  there are 538 in total so the candidate  that gets the support of at least  270 electors becomes president  this was a picture in 2016 when hillary  clinton  lost to donald trump after receiving 227  votes  to his 304. she lost the electoral  college but she won the popular vote  receiving almost 3 million more votes  nationally  than donald trump ultimately  presidential elections are decided by  swing states shown here in gold these  are the battleground areas  where a win has a critical impact on the  result  trump's victory in 2016 happened because  he won michigan  ohio pennsylvania and wisconsin from the  democrats  joe biden will be attempting to reverse  those losses  while keeping hold of his own heartlands  including nevada  and minnesota well americans won't just  be voting for the next president they'll  also be vote  electing new members of the senate and  the house of representatives the two  chambers  that make up the legislative branch of  the u.s government  in the house all 435 seats are  up for election this year the  republicans have some catching up to do  here  after the midterm elections in 2018  which saw the democrats gain  40 seats meanwhile in the senate there  are a hundred seats but only 35 will be  voted on in 2018 the democrats success  in the house came at the expense  of republican victory in the senate  it can take days for every vote to be  counted but it is usually clear  who the winner is by the following  morning if biden wins he won't  immediately replace trump there is a  transition period  before the new president is officially  inaugurated  on the 20th of january
 

